# Plaid Connect Development Rules

## Commit Message Format
Always use conventional commits to enable automated PR tracking:

### Format
```
<type>(<scope>): <description>

[optional body]

[optional footer]
```

### Types
- `feat` - New features
- `fix` - Bug fixes
- `chore` - Maintenance tasks (deps, config, etc.)
- `docs` - Documentation only changes
- `refactor` - Code refactoring (no functionality change)
- `security` - Security improvements
- `perf` - Performance improvements
- `test` - Adding or updating tests

### Common Scopes
- `plaid` - Plaid integration features
- `auth` - Authentication
- `api` - API endpoints
- `db` - Database changes
- `ui` - User interface components
- `ci` - CI/CD and workflows
- `config` - Configuration changes

### Examples
```
feat(plaid): add account linking flow
fix(auth): resolve token refresh issue
chore(deps): update plaid sdk to latest version
security(auth): implement rate limiting on login
docs(api): update authentication documentation
refactor(ui): simplify account selection component
```

## PR Description Template
When asked to generate a PR description, include:

1. **Summary**: Brief overview of changes
2. **Changes by Category**: Group commits by type
3. **Testing**: Note what was tested in staging
4. **Breaking Changes**: Highlight any breaking changes
5. **Affected Systems**: List integrations affected (Plaid, auth, etc.)

## Development Workflow
- All feature work → `staging` branch
- Test thoroughly in staging environment
- Batch merge to `main` when ready (GitHub Actions will track changes)
- CI automatically maintains draft PR from staging → main

## Code Quality
- Follow existing patterns in the codebase
- Add TypeScript types for all new code
- Handle errors gracefully with proper logging
- Use security layers for sensitive operations
- Document complex business logic

